Evaluating multi-agent interactions requires robust metrics for collaboration and failure analysis. Studies on LLM   
agent groups reveal that simulated consensus often overstates agreement compared to human deliberation, highlighting 
measurement asymmetries in multi-agent evaluation[^2609.20543]. In economic simulations, reinforcement learning      
agents in repeated games can converge to collusive equilibria, necessitating reward-shaping interventions like CURB  
to unwind retaliatory collusion and restore competitive baselines[^2609.20548]. Finally, peer-free mutual evaluation 
mechanisms have been developed to incentivize truthful reporting between worker and critic agents, enabling unbiased 
information scoring without requiring external ground-truth references[^2609.20789].                                 
                                                                                                                     

